Improve enemy tactical positioning while preserving the existing game’s unit statistics and visual
  style.

  Implement the following enemy-only tactical behaviors:

  - Archers try to maintain an effective distance from nearby melee threats.
  - An archer retreats or repositions when a swordsman enters a configurable danger radius.
  - Archers must not retreat outside the map or continually oscillate between positions.
  - Swordsmen try to place themselves between allied archers and nearby threats.
  - Units maintain modest separation so they do not stack directly on top of each other.
  - Repositioning should not prevent a unit from attacking when it already has a good firing position.
  - Explicit strategic orders such as defending or rallying should constrain where units are allowed to
  reposition.

  Use steering or lightweight movement adjustments appropriate for this simple open map. Do not introduce
  a heavyweight pathfinding system unless the existing map requires it.

  Add deterministic tests for:

  - Archers retreating from close melee units
  - Archers holding position when safely within attack range
  - Archers resuming attacks after repositioning
  - Swordsmen moving to screen nearby archers
  - Units separating when overlapping
  - Units staying within map boundaries
  - No rapid position oscillation in a controlled scenario
  - No change to player-controlled unit behavior

  After implementation:

  1. Run all automated tests.
  2. Run a syntax or compilation check.
  3. Test swordsmen versus archers, mixed groups versus mixed groups, and combat near map edges.
  4. Confirm movement speed, attack range, damage, and cooldown values remain unchanged.
  5. Take screenshots only under /work/screenshots, inspect each with view_image, and report clipping,
  stacking, jitter, or other visual problems.
  6. Report the tactical behavior observed and any limitations.

  Do not implement coordinated attack waves or adaptive production in this step. Do not commit changes.
